Z||000000||fierospeeder||08-02-2000||10:30 PM||||Im going to buy a mig welder soon, and was wondering which one i should buy. <P>I have three choices, but 2 different brands.<BR>120volt migs.<P>Sears has a new craftsman mig welder which has a better duty cycle then the other ones.  479 dollars, and i get 10% off during members day.  <BR>If someone owns the cheaper mig that sears sells does it include instructions on what setting it should be on?<P>The lincoln 100, i seen it at homedepot for 310 bucks, and i would have to buy the 100 dollar conversion.  Sears also sells in their catalog, plus my 10%<P>The lincoln mig-pak 10, which is already mig welding ready but for 495 bucks from eastwood.<P>All the stats for the welders are the same except sears has a 20% duty cycle at 105amps, and lincoln has 20%@88amps for both models.<P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||08-02-2000||11:59 PM||||I would be partial to the lincoln's. But I use an off brand I got for around $270. Whatever you decide be sure to get what ever you need to get to use regular wire and gas. They probably come set up to run flux cored wire and no gas. Which is ok for doing fence repair but not for working on cars. It burns real hot and doesnt get a decent weld. In strength or apperience. I know there are other opinions. This is just mine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||08-03-2000||07:18 AM||||I have to agree wth TOM on this one. I've got the Lincoln and it's great. I used to use an off brand and thought it was good until I tried the Lincoln. I don't know what it was but it was like night and day. <P>One very good thing about a Lincoln is the parts availability. Any welding shop will have the normal wear parts in stock. With an off brand (like the one I was first using) you may have to order it from the manufacturer. <P>The Miller brand is also a geat item, a little more than the Lincoln but maybe a litle better.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
